[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186064153 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4fdd627b-10f3-56fb-8e8b-56c0cb38ac41"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186064272 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-99da720f-6132-59c4-9760-cafcb4cb789a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-99da720f-6132-59c4-9760-cafcb4cb789a" :question/id #uuid "99da720f-6132-59c4-9760-cafcb4cb789a" :question/math-academy-id "q-72106" :question/problem "Joseph is measuring the weight gain of his pet puppy by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in days, since he started his observations:\n\n- $W(t)$ - The puppy's weight, in kilograms\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the puppy's weight is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $W(t)$ and $r(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "The function $W(t)$ gives the weight of the puppy after $t$ days. Then,\n\n$$\n{W}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}W(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }W}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${W}^{′}(t)$ gives the rate at which the weight of the puppy increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $W(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${W}^{′}(t)=r(t).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cc125dcb-0e32-5fe9-99db-6daa9af7ad33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cc125dcb-0e32-5fe9-99db-6daa9af7ad33" :answer-field/id #uuid "cc125dcb-0e32-5fe9-99db-6daa9af7ad33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1b410e34-af3e-5a01-933c-fbe6349f45e1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9b37480b-e617-5540-824f-b3833f4eb298" "answer-7481abc5-b42c-5b1f-8b53-c6646dad9e85" "answer-a85f2065-b5b6-5ad1-8dd5-e1cb912ecdde" "answer-33d00ba0-1a85-58f1-8992-73d818d55ac7" "answer-1b410e34-af3e-5a01-933c-fbe6349f45e1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b37480b-e617-5540-824f-b3833f4eb298" :answer/id #uuid "9b37480b-e617-5540-824f-b3833f4eb298" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "W(t)=\\frac{1}{{r}^{′}(t)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7481abc5-b42c-5b1f-8b53-c6646dad9e85" :answer/id #uuid "7481abc5-b42c-5b1f-8b53-c6646dad9e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "W(t)=r(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a85f2065-b5b6-5ad1-8dd5-e1cb912ecdde" :answer/id #uuid "a85f2065-b5b6-5ad1-8dd5-e1cb912ecdde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "W(t)={r}^{′}(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33d00ba0-1a85-58f1-8992-73d818d55ac7" :answer/id #uuid "33d00ba0-1a85-58f1-8992-73d818d55ac7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{W}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{r(t)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b410e34-af3e-5a01-933c-fbe6349f45e1" :answer/id #uuid "1b410e34-af3e-5a01-933c-fbe6349f45e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{W}^{′}(t)=r(t)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4fdd627b-10f3-56fb-8e8b-56c0cb38ac41" :question/id #uuid "4fdd627b-10f3-56fb-8e8b-56c0cb38ac41" :question/math-academy-id "q-112093" :question/problem "In a factory, the cost of producing an item depends on the number of items produced per day. The function $U(x)$ models the cost, in dollars, to produce an item if the factory is producing $x$ items per day. What is the best interpretation of ${U}^{′}(500)=0.01?$" :question/worked-solution "The function ${U}^{′}(x)$ represents the rate at which the cost changes when the factory produces $x$ items per day. \n\nTherefore, ${U}^{′}(500)=0.01$ means:\n\n$\\,$ *When the factory produces $500$ items per day, the cost per item increases at a rate of $0.01$ dollars per item*" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-43e5e2b2-a362-5eae-bdb3-072556508ad1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-43e5e2b2-a362-5eae-bdb3-072556508ad1" :answer-field/id #uuid "43e5e2b2-a362-